WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.873 --> 00:00:07.140
"Guilty."

2
00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.977
"Guilty."

3
00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:14.397
"Guilty."

4
00:00:16.634 --> 00:00:17.901
"Guilty."

5
00:00:19.303 --> 00:00:20.570
"Guilty."

6
00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:23.657
"Guilty."

7
00:00:25.726 --> 00:00:26.993
"Guilty."

8
00:00:28.062 --> 00:00:29.329
"Guilty."

9
00:00:30.523 --> 00:00:31.790
"Guilty."

10
00:00:35.778 --> 00:00:38.488
"Not guilty."

11
00:00:38.489 --> 00:00:39.990
"Guilty."

12
00:00:39.991 --> 00:00:41.741
Boy, how do you like that?

13
00:00:41.742 --> 00:00:44.286
Oh, and another chap
flaps his wings.

14
00:00:44.287 --> 00:00:47.122
All right, who was it?
Come on. I want to know.

15
00:00:47.123 --> 00:00:49.457
Excuse me.
This was a secret ballot.

16
00:00:49.458 --> 00:00:51.084
We all agreed on that, no?

17
00:00:51.085 --> 00:00:53.295
If the gentleman wanted
to remain secret...

18
00:00:53.296 --> 00:00:55.255
Secret? What do you mean secret?

19
00:00:55.256 --> 00:00:58.566
There are no secrets in a
jury room. I know who it was.

20
00:00:59.218 --> 00:01:01.219
Brother, you really are something.

21
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.305
You sit here, vote guilty
like the rest of us...

22
00:01:03.306 --> 00:01:06.683
and then some golden-voiced preacher
starts tearin' your poor heart out...

23
00:01:06.684 --> 00:01:10.061
about some underprivileged kid just
couldn't help becomin' a murderer...

24
00:01:10.062 --> 00:01:11.646
and you change your vote.

25
00:01:11.647 --> 00:01:13.857
If that isn't the most sick...

26
00:01:13.858 --> 00:01:17.611
Why don't you drop a quarter
in his collection box?

27
00:01:17.612 --> 00:01:19.654
Oh, now just wait a minute.

28
00:01:19.655 --> 00:01:21.323
Listen, you can't talk
to me like that.

29
00:01:21.324 --> 00:01:23.241
- No. Who do you think you are?
- Calm down. Calm down.

30
00:01:23.242 --> 00:01:25.660
No. Who does he think he is?
Did you hear him?

31
00:01:25.661 --> 00:01:27.075
It doesn't matter. He's
very excitable. Sit down.

32
00:01:27.101 --> 00:01:29.005
Excitable? You bet I'm excitable!

33
00:01:29.006 --> 00:01:31.925
We're trying to put a guilty man
in the chair where he belongs!

34
00:01:32.126 --> 00:01:34.878
Someone starts telling us fairy
tales, and we're listenin'!

35
00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:36.713
Hey, uh, come on, huh?

36
00:01:36.714 --> 00:01:38.715
What made you change your vote?

37
00:01:38.716 --> 00:01:40.425
He didn't change his vote.

38
00:01:40.426 --> 00:01:41.676
I did.

39
00:01:41.677 --> 00:01:43.720
- Oh, fine.
- I knew it.

40
00:01:43.721 --> 00:01:45.764
Would you like me to tell you why?

41
00:01:45.765 --> 00:01:47.724
No, I wouldn't like
you to tell me why.

42
00:01:47.725 --> 00:01:50.352
I'd like to make it clear
anyway, if you don't mind.

43
00:01:50.353 --> 00:01:51.937
Do we have to listen to this?

44
00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:53.772
The man wants to talk.

45
00:01:53.773 --> 00:01:55.440
Thank you.

46
00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:58.860
This gentleman has been
standing alone against us.

47
00:01:58.861 --> 00:02:01.196
Now, he doesn't say
the boy is not guilty.

48
00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:03.239
He just isn't sure.

49
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.159
Well, it's not easy to stand alone
against the ridicule of others.

50
00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.371
So he gambled for support,
and I gave it to him.

51
00:02:09.372 --> 00:02:11.164
I respect his motives.

52
00:02:11.165 --> 00:02:15.794
The boy on trial is probably guilty,
but, uh, I want to hear more.

53
00:02:15.795 --> 00:02:17.671
Right now, the vote is 10 to two.

54
00:02:17.672 --> 00:02:20.006
I'm talking here! You have
no right to leave this room!

55
00:02:20.007 --> 00:02:21.967
He can't hear you. He never will.

56
00:02:21.968 --> 00:02:23.651
Let's sit down.

57
00:02:27.723 --> 00:02:30.016
Shall we continue?

58
00:02:30.017 --> 00:02:32.310
Well, I... I think we
ought to take a break.

59
00:02:32.311 --> 00:02:36.523
You know, one man's inside,
and I think we ought to wait for him.

60
00:02:36.524 --> 00:02:39.567
Okay.

61
00:02:39.568 --> 00:02:41.419
Looks like we're really
hung up here, huh?

62
00:02:42.697 --> 00:02:45.131
And that thing with the old man,
that was pretty unexpected.

63
00:02:47.118 --> 00:02:49.677
I wish I could figure out some
way we could break it up.

64
00:02:52.707 --> 00:02:56.251
You know, in advertising...
I told you I worked in an agency.

65
00:02:56.252 --> 00:02:57.335
Mm-hmm.

66
00:02:57.336 --> 00:02:59.421
Well, there's some pretty
strange people working there.

67
00:02:59.422 --> 00:03:01.589
Well, they're not strange
really, I guess.

68
00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:04.300
It's just that they have peculiar
ways of expressing themselves.

69
00:03:04.301 --> 00:03:05.635
You know what I mean.

70
00:03:05.636 --> 00:03:08.036
Of course, I suppose it's the same
in your business too, huh?

71
00:03:08.597 --> 00:03:10.181
What do you do?

72
00:03:10.182 --> 00:03:12.434
- I'm a watchmaker.
- Oh, really?

73
00:03:12.435 --> 00:03:15.729
Well, I imagine the finest watchmakers
in the world come from Europe, huh?

74
00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.815
Anyway, as I was telling you, in an agency,
when we reach a point like this...

75
00:03:18.816 --> 00:03:22.402
I'm telling him about, in an ad agency, when
a point like this is reached in a meeting...

76
00:03:22.403 --> 00:03:25.113
there's always some character
ready with an idea, see.

77
00:03:25.114 --> 00:03:27.157
And it kills me.

78
00:03:27.158 --> 00:03:28.742
It's the weirdest
thing in the world...

79
00:03:28.743 --> 00:03:32.162
the way they sometimes precede their
idea with a little phrase, see, like...

80
00:03:32.163 --> 00:03:35.165
Some account exec will
get up, and he'll say, uh...

81
00:03:35.166 --> 00:03:37.083
"Okay, here's an idea.

82
00:03:37.084 --> 00:03:40.795
Let's, uh, run it up the flagpole
and see if anyone salutes it."

83
00:03:40.796 --> 00:03:44.007
I mean, it's idiotic,
but it's funny, huh?

84
00:03:44.008 --> 00:03:48.611
I, uh... I got a little
excited back there. I...

85
00:03:49.513 --> 00:03:51.364
didn't mean to get nasty.

86
00:03:52.141 --> 00:03:56.035
I'm glad you're not one of those lets
these emotional appeals influence him.

87
00:03:57.104 --> 00:03:59.455
Uh...

88
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.620
I don't know what's the matter with
that fan. Can't seem to get it to start.

89
00:04:22.004 --> 00:04:24.022
Eh!

90
00:04:25.049 --> 00:04:27.342
Hey, you a salesman?

91
00:04:27.343 --> 00:04:28.943
I'm an architect.

92
00:04:29.678 --> 00:04:31.096
Hmm.

93
00:04:31.097 --> 00:04:33.448
You know what the soft sell is?

94
00:04:36.268 --> 00:04:38.286
Boy, you got it. Believe me.

95
00:04:39.855 --> 00:04:41.664
I got a different technique.

96
00:04:42.358 --> 00:04:44.234
Laughs, drinks, jokes.

97
00:04:44.235 --> 00:04:46.586
Tricks. You know?

98
00:04:47.321 --> 00:04:48.630
Yeah.

99
00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:52.300
"Hit 'em where they live."
That's my motto.

100
00:04:53.619 --> 00:04:56.471
I made 27 grand last
year sellin' marmalade.

101
00:04:57.206 --> 00:04:59.807
That's not bad. I mean,
you know, considering marmalade.

102
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:04.812
Hey, what are you gettin'
outta this... kicks?

103
00:05:06.882 --> 00:05:09.551
Or did somebody bump you on the head one
time and you haven't gotten over it?

104
00:05:09.552 --> 00:05:11.361
Maybe.

105
00:05:12.263 --> 00:05:14.889
You know, you do-gooders
are all alike.

106
00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:17.659
You're always blowing your stacks
over some guy that fanned.

107
00:05:20.646 --> 00:05:22.580
But what are you
wasting our time for?

108
00:05:23.232 --> 00:05:27.377
Why don't you donate five dollars to the
cause, and maybe it'll make you feel better.

109
00:05:30.573 --> 00:05:32.907
This kid is guilty, pal.

110
00:05:32.908 --> 00:05:35.410
It's as plain as the
nose on your face.

111
00:05:35.411 --> 00:05:37.745
So why don't we stop
wasting our time here?

112
00:05:37.746 --> 00:05:40.748
We're gonna all get sore throats
if we keep it up, you know?

113
00:05:40.749 --> 00:05:43.430
What difference does it make if you
get it here or at the ball game?

114
00:05:44.086 --> 00:05:47.088
Oh, no difference, pal.

115
00:05:47.089 --> 00:05:48.982
No difference at all.

116
00:06:00.811 --> 00:06:02.770
Nice bunch of guys, huh?

117
00:06:02.771 --> 00:06:05.148
Oh, they're about the
same as anyone else.

118
00:06:05.149 --> 00:06:08.042
Phew! Boy, what a murderous day.

119
00:06:08.944 --> 00:06:11.070
You think we'll be much longer?

120
00:06:11.071 --> 00:06:12.780
I don't know.

121
00:06:12.781 --> 00:06:14.782
Aw, he's guilty for sure.

122
00:06:14.783 --> 00:06:16.409
Not a doubt in the whole world.

123
00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.053
We should...
Should've been done already.

124
00:06:21.207 --> 00:06:22.540
Oh, I don't mind, you know.

125
00:06:22.541 --> 00:06:25.059
Hmm. Beats workin'.

126
00:06:28.631 --> 00:06:29.897
Uh...

127
00:06:32.176 --> 00:06:34.344
You think he's not guilty, huh?

128
00:06:34.345 --> 00:06:36.279
I don't know. It's possible.

129
00:06:36.931 --> 00:06:40.808
Well, I don't know you, but I'm bettin'
you never been wronger in your life.

130
00:06:40.809 --> 00:06:43.311
You're wasting your time.
You ought to wrap it up.

131
00:06:43.312 --> 00:06:45.580
Supposing you were the
one who was on trial.

132
00:06:46.315 --> 00:06:48.274
Well, I'm not used to supposing.

133
00:06:48.275 --> 00:06:52.862
I'm just a workingman. My boss
does the supposing. But...

134
00:06:52.863 --> 00:06:54.464
Well, I'll try one.

135
00:06:55.574 --> 00:07:01.346
Supposing you talk us all out of this, and,
the kid really did knife his father. Huh?

136
00:07:10.172 --> 00:07:11.547
Are you ready?

137
00:07:11.548 --> 00:07:12.840
Hup.

138
00:07:12.841 --> 00:07:13.841
That one.

139
00:07:13.842 --> 00:07:15.610
Sorry, blue eyes.

140
00:07:16.428 --> 00:07:17.595
- Well, where else...
- Okay, fellas, let's take our seats.

141
00:07:17.596 --> 00:07:19.931
- Well, where else...
- Okay, fellas, let's take our seats.

142
00:07:19.932 --> 00:07:21.933
Looks like we'll be
here for dinner, huh?

143
00:07:21.934 --> 00:07:24.394
Okay, now let's...
Let's get down to business.

144
00:07:24.395 --> 00:07:25.853
Now, who wants to start it off?

145
00:07:25.854 --> 00:07:27.355
- I would.
- Okay, go.

146
00:07:27.356 --> 00:07:29.524
You down there.

147
00:07:29.525 --> 00:07:33.486
The old man who lived downstairs says he
heard the kid yell out, "I'm gonna kill ya."

148
00:07:33.487 --> 00:07:36.197
A second later, he heard
the body hit the floor.

149
00:07:36.198 --> 00:07:40.159
Now, he ran to the door and he saw the kid
running down the stairs and out of the house.

150
00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.619
What does that mean to you?

151
00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:43.273
I was wondering how
clearly the old man could

152
00:07:43.274 --> 00:07:45.081
have heard the boy's voice
through the ceiling.

153
00:07:45.082 --> 00:07:47.208
He didn't hear it through the ceiling.
The window was open.

154
00:07:47.209 --> 00:07:49.127
So was the one upstairs.
It was a hot night, remember?

155
00:07:49.128 --> 00:07:50.545
Well, it was another apartment.

156
00:07:50.546 --> 00:07:53.214
It's not that easy to identify a voice,
particularly a shouting voice.

157
00:07:53.215 --> 00:07:54.882
- He identified it in court.
- That's right.

158
00:07:54.883 --> 00:07:56.426
And don't forget the
lady across the street.

159
00:07:56.427 --> 00:07:59.304
She looked right in the open window
and saw the boy stab his father.

160
00:07:59.305 --> 00:08:01.848
- Now, I mean, isn't that enough for you?
- No, it isn't.

161
00:08:01.849 --> 00:08:03.891
Boy, how do you like this guy, huh?

162
00:08:03.892 --> 00:08:05.810
It's like talking
into a dead phone.

163
00:08:05.811 --> 00:08:09.063
She said she saw the killing through
the windows of a moving elevated train.

164
00:08:09.064 --> 00:08:12.650
There were six cars on the train. She saw
the killing through the last two cars.

165
00:08:12.651 --> 00:08:14.569
She remembered the most
insignificant details.

166
00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:16.696
I don't see how you
can argue with that.

167
00:08:16.697 --> 00:08:21.342
Has anybody here any idea
how long it would take an el...

168
00:08:29.918 --> 00:08:31.586
Hey!

169
00:08:31.587 --> 00:08:33.187
This isn't a game.

170
00:08:37.384 --> 00:08:39.719
- Did you see him?
- Hey.

171
00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:41.554
- The nerve.
- All right, listen...

172
00:08:41.555 --> 00:08:44.891
- The absolute nerve.
- Forget it. It's not important.

173
00:08:44.892 --> 00:08:46.934
- "This isn't a game"?
- Calm down.

174
00:08:46.935 --> 00:08:50.355
- Who does he think he is?
- I'm telling you, forget it now.

175
00:08:50.356 --> 00:08:53.775
Has anybody any idea how long
it takes an elevated train...

176
00:08:53.776 --> 00:08:55.693
going at medium speed
to pass a given point?

177
00:08:55.694 --> 00:08:57.653
- What has that to do with anything?
- How long? Take a guess.

178
00:08:57.654 --> 00:09:00.406
- I wouldn't have the slightest idea.
- What do you think?

179
00:09:00.407 --> 00:09:02.383
I don't know. Maybe 10, 12 seconds?

180
00:09:03.285 --> 00:09:05.036
I think that's a pretty
good guess. Anyone else?

181
00:09:05.037 --> 00:09:07.914
- That sounds right to me.
- Come on. What's the guessing game for?

182
00:09:07.915 --> 00:09:09.832
- What do you say?
- Ten seconds is about right.

183
00:09:09.833 --> 00:09:11.834
All right. Say 10 seconds.
What are you getting at?

184
00:09:11.835 --> 00:09:16.964
This. It takes a six-car el train
10 seconds to pass a given point.

185
00:09:16.965 --> 00:09:19.300
Now let's say the given
point is the open window

186
00:09:19.326 --> 00:09:21.302
of the room where the
killing took place.

187
00:09:21.303 --> 00:09:24.680
You can reach out of that window
and almost touch the el tracks, right?

188
00:09:24.681 --> 00:09:26.265
Now let me ask you this.

189
00:09:26.266 --> 00:09:28.601
Has anyone here ever lived
near the el tracks?

190
00:09:28.602 --> 00:09:32.313
Well, uh, I just finished painting an
apartment that overlooked an el line.

191
00:09:32.314 --> 00:09:34.065
- I was there for three days.
- What was it like?

192
00:09:34.066 --> 00:09:35.316
- What do you mean?
- Noisy?

193
00:09:35.317 --> 00:09:37.944
Oh, brother. Don't matter though.
We were all punchy.

194
00:09:37.945 --> 00:09:40.613
I lived in a second-floor
apartment near the el line once.

195
00:09:40.614 --> 00:09:43.758
When the window was open and the train
goes by, the noise is almost unbearable.

196
00:09:43.784 --> 00:09:44.910
You can hardly hear yourself think.

197
00:09:44.936 --> 00:09:46.661
All right, you can't
hear yourself think.

198
00:09:46.662 --> 00:09:49.163
- Will you get to the point?
- I will. Now just a minute.

199
00:09:49.164 --> 00:09:52.875
Let's take two pieces of testimony
and try to put them together.

200
00:09:52.876 --> 00:09:57.046
First. The old man in the
apartment downstairs.

201
00:09:57.047 --> 00:09:59.715
He says he heard the boy say
"I'm gonna kill you"...

202
00:09:59.716 --> 00:10:02.552
and a split second later
heard a body hit the floor.

203
00:10:02.553 --> 00:10:04.220
- One second later, right?
- That's right.

204
00:10:04.221 --> 00:10:10.309
Second. The woman across the street swore
positively she looked out of the window...

205
00:10:10.310 --> 00:10:13.729
and saw the killing through the last two
cars of the passing elevated train, right?

206
00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.149
- The last two cars.
- What are you giving us here?

207
00:10:16.150 --> 00:10:17.567
Now, just a minute.

208
00:10:17.568 --> 00:10:22.088
We've agreed that it takes 10 seconds
for a train to pass a given point.

209
00:10:22.823 --> 00:10:26.242
Since the woman saw the killing
through the last two cars...

210
00:10:26.243 --> 00:10:31.289
we can assume that the body hit the
floor just as the train went by.

211
00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:35.835
Therefore, the train had been
roaring by the old man's window...

212
00:10:35.836 --> 00:10:39.422
a full 10 seconds before
the body hit the floor.

213
00:10:39.423 --> 00:10:41.716
The old man, according
to his own testimony...

214
00:10:41.717 --> 00:10:44.719
"I'm gonna kill you," body hitting
the floor a split second later...

215
00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.263
would have had to hear the
boy make this statement...

216
00:10:47.264 --> 00:10:49.223
with the el roaring past his nose.

217
00:10:49.224 --> 00:10:50.850
It's not possible he
could've heard it.

218
00:10:50.851 --> 00:10:53.102
- That's idiotic. Of course he heard it.
- Do you think he could've heard it?

219
00:10:53.103 --> 00:10:55.563
He said he yelled at the top of his
voice. That's good enough for me.

220
00:10:55.564 --> 00:10:57.899
Even if he heard something, he still
couldn't have identified the voice.

221
00:10:57.900 --> 00:10:59.108
With the el roaring by?

222
00:10:59.109 --> 00:11:01.569
You're talking about a matter of seconds!
Nobody can be that accurate.

223
00:11:01.570 --> 00:11:06.282
I think testimony that could put a boy into
the electric chair should be that accurate.

224
00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:10.411
You know something? I don't
think he could've heard it.

225
00:11:10.412 --> 00:11:12.830
Maybe he didn't hear it.
I mean, with all that el noise.

226
00:11:12.831 --> 00:11:14.790
Oh, what are you
people talking about?

227
00:11:14.791 --> 00:11:16.918
Well, it stands to reason
he couldn't have heard it.

228
00:11:16.919 --> 00:11:18.920
Why should he lie?
What's he got to gain?

229
00:11:18.921 --> 00:11:21.214
Attention maybe.

230
00:11:21.215 --> 00:11:23.216
You keep coming in with
these bright sayings!

231
00:11:23.217 --> 00:11:26.344
Why don't you send 'em in to a paper?
They pay three dollars apiece.

232
00:11:26.345 --> 00:11:28.654
What are you talking
to him like that for?

233
00:11:29.515 --> 00:11:33.017
Guy talks like that to an old man really
ought to get stepped on, you know?

234
00:11:33.018 --> 00:11:34.952
You ought to have
more respect, mister.

235
00:11:36.063 --> 00:11:40.541
If you say stuff like that to him
again, I'm gonna lay you out.

236
00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.454
Now you go ahead. You...
You say anything you like.

237
00:11:53.455 --> 00:11:57.583
Why do you think the
old man might lie?

238
00:11:57.584 --> 00:12:01.103
It was just that I looked at
him for a very long time.

239
00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.966
The seams of his jacket was split,
under the shoulder.

240
00:12:04.967 --> 00:12:06.634
Or did you notice that?

241
00:12:06.635 --> 00:12:09.220
I mean, to come to court like that.

242
00:12:09.221 --> 00:12:10.988
Why...

243
00:12:12.015 --> 00:12:15.977
He was a very old man
in a torn jacket.

244
00:12:15.978 --> 00:12:18.954
And he walked very
slowly to the stand.

245
00:12:19.606 --> 00:12:23.734
He was dragging his left leg
and trying to hide it...

246
00:12:23.735 --> 00:12:25.503
because he was ashamed.

247
00:12:26.530 --> 00:12:30.049
I think I know this man
better than anyone here.

248
00:12:30.826 --> 00:12:36.330
This is a quiet, frightened,
insignificant old man who...

249
00:12:36.331 --> 00:12:38.791
who has been nothing
all his life...

250
00:12:38.792 --> 00:12:41.294
who has never had recognition...

251
00:12:41.295 --> 00:12:43.479
or his name in the newspapers.

252
00:12:44.047 --> 00:12:46.340
Nobody knows him.

253
00:12:46.341 --> 00:12:48.467
Nobody quotes him.

254
00:12:48.468 --> 00:12:52.530
Nobody seeks his
advice after 75 years.

255
00:12:53.265 --> 00:12:57.243
Gentlemen, that's a very
sad thing... to be nothing.

256
00:12:57.853 --> 00:13:02.289
A man like this needs to be
quoted, to be listened to.

257
00:13:02.816 --> 00:13:04.859
To be quoted just once.

258
00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.836
Very important to him.

259
00:13:07.237 --> 00:13:09.739
It would be so hard for him to
recede into the background...

260
00:13:09.740 --> 00:13:11.532
- Wait a minute.
- When there was a chance...

261
00:13:11.533 --> 00:13:14.493
What are you trying to do... tell us he'd
lie just so he could be important once?

262
00:13:14.494 --> 00:13:17.204
No. He wouldn't really lie.

263
00:13:17.205 --> 00:13:20.750
But perhaps he made himself
believe he heard those words...

264
00:13:20.751 --> 00:13:23.044
and recognized the boy's face.

265
00:13:23.045 --> 00:13:25.421
That's the most fantastic
story I've ever heard.

266
00:13:25.422 --> 00:13:28.566
How can you make up a thing like that?
What do you know about it?

267
00:13:37.517 --> 00:13:39.644
Oh, uh, does anybody
want a cough drop?

268
00:13:39.645 --> 00:13:41.979
- No, thanks.
- I'll take one.

269
00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:43.381
You can say what you like.

270
00:13:43.382 --> 00:13:45.566
I still don't see how anybody
can think he's not guilty.

271
00:13:45.567 --> 00:13:48.152
There's something else I'd like
to talk about for a minute.

272
00:13:48.153 --> 00:13:49.862
Thanks.

273
00:13:49.863 --> 00:13:53.240
I think we've proved that the old man couldn't
have heard the boy say "I'm gonna kill you."

274
00:13:53.241 --> 00:13:54.909
- But supposing...
- You didn't prove it at all.

275
00:13:54.910 --> 00:13:57.787
- What are you talking about?
- Supposing he really did hear it.

276
00:13:57.788 --> 00:14:00.289
This phrase, how many times
have all of us used it?

277
00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:01.874
Probably thousands.

278
00:14:01.875 --> 00:14:03.584
"I could kill you
for that, darling."

279
00:14:03.585 --> 00:14:05.586
"Junior, you do that once more,
and I'm gonna kill you."

280
00:14:05.587 --> 00:14:06.962
"Get in there, Rocky,
and kill him."

281
00:14:06.963 --> 00:14:08.923
We say it every day. That doesn't
mean we're really gonna kill anybody.

282
00:14:08.924 --> 00:14:10.800
Wait a minute. What are you
trying to give us here?

283
00:14:10.801 --> 00:14:14.595
The phrase was "I'm gonna kill you."
The kid yelled it at the top of his lungs.

284
00:14:14.596 --> 00:14:16.013
Don't tell me he didn't mean it.

285
00:14:16.014 --> 00:14:18.391
Anybody says a thing like that
the way he said it, they mean it.

286
00:14:18.392 --> 00:14:19.934
Well, gee, now I don't know.

287
00:14:19.935 --> 00:14:24.730
I remember I was arguing with a guy I work
next to at the bank a couple weeks ago.

288
00:14:24.731 --> 00:14:26.565
He called me an idiot,
so I yelled at him.

289
00:14:26.566 --> 00:14:29.610
Now listen! This guy's trying to make
you believe things that aren't so.

290
00:14:29.611 --> 00:14:31.779
The kid said he was gonna
kill him, and he did kill him.

291
00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:34.824
Let me ask you this. Do you really think the
boy would shout out a thing like that...

292
00:14:34.825 --> 00:14:36.701
so the whole neighborhood
could hear him?

293
00:14:36.702 --> 00:14:38.619
I don't think so.
He's much too bright for that.

294
00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:43.457
Bright? He's a common ignorant slob.
He don't even speak good English.

295
00:14:43.458 --> 00:14:46.393
"He doesn't even
speak good English."

296
00:14:48.755 --> 00:14:52.633
Mr. Foreman, I'd like to
change my vote to not guilty.

297
00:14:52.634 --> 00:14:54.135
You what?

298
00:14:54.136 --> 00:14:56.654
- You heard me.
- Are you sure?

299
00:14:57.472 --> 00:14:58.973
Yeah, I'm sure.

300
00:14:58.974 --> 00:15:01.517
The vote is nine to three,
favor of guilty.

301
00:15:01.518 --> 00:15:04.411
Well, if this isn't
the living end. Huh?

302
00:15:08.358 --> 00:15:11.210
What are you basing it on...
the stories this guy made up?

303
00:15:11.737 --> 00:15:14.155
You ought to write for one of
those kooky detective magazines.

304
00:15:14.156 --> 00:15:16.073
You'd make a fortune.

305
00:15:16.074 --> 00:15:19.593
For crying out loud. The kid's own
lawyer knew he didn't stand a chance.

306
00:15:19.995 --> 00:15:21.662
Right from the beginning,
his own lawyer knew it.

307
00:15:21.663 --> 00:15:23.539
You could see it.

308
00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:27.226
Boy, oh, boy. I'm tellin' you,
this guy here is really something.

309
00:15:27.961 --> 00:15:29.837
Look, the kid had a
lawyer, didn't he?

310
00:15:29.838 --> 00:15:32.757
He presented his case, not you.
How come you got so much to say?

311
00:15:32.758 --> 00:15:35.676
- Look, lawyers aren't infallible, you know.
- Baltimore, please. Huh?

312
00:15:35.677 --> 00:15:37.470
He was court-appointed.

313
00:15:37.471 --> 00:15:39.388
- What's that supposed to mean?
- It could mean a lot of things.

314
00:15:39.389 --> 00:15:42.725
It could mean he didn't want the case,
or he resented being appointed.

315
00:15:42.726 --> 00:15:44.643
It's the kind of case that
brings him nothing...

316
00:15:44.644 --> 00:15:47.688
no money, no glory, not even
much chance of winning.

317
00:15:47.689 --> 00:15:50.691
That's not a very promising
situation for a young lawyer.

318
00:15:50.692 --> 00:15:53.694
He'd really have to believe in his client
to put up any kind of a good case...

319
00:15:53.695 --> 00:15:55.780
and, as you pointed out a minute
ago, obviously he didn't.

320
00:15:55.781 --> 00:15:58.699
Of course he didn't. Huh.

321
00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.076
Who the heck could?

322
00:16:01.077 --> 00:16:04.079
Except maybe some guy's
mother or somebody. I...

323
00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.415
Ah, look. Will you
look at the time, huh?

324
00:16:06.416 --> 00:16:09.043
- Come on. For crying out...
- Pardon me.

325
00:16:09.044 --> 00:16:11.462
- I have made some notes here...
- Notes.

326
00:16:11.463 --> 00:16:14.173
And I would like to...
please, to say something.

327
00:16:14.174 --> 00:16:16.509
- Yeah.
- I have been listening very carefully.

328
00:16:16.510 --> 00:16:21.013
And, uh, it seems to me that this man
has some very good points to make.

329
00:16:21.014 --> 00:16:25.726
From what was presented at the trial,
the boy looks guilty on the surface.

330
00:16:25.727 --> 00:16:29.897
- But maybe if we go deeper?
- Oh, come on, will ya?

331
00:16:29.898 --> 00:16:32.274
There is a question I
would like to ask.

332
00:16:32.275 --> 00:16:35.653
Let us assume that the boy
really did commit the murder.

333
00:16:35.654 --> 00:16:39.073
Now, this happened at
10 minutes after 12:00.

334
00:16:39.074 --> 00:16:41.617
Now, how was he
caught by the police?

335
00:16:41.618 --> 00:16:46.205
He came back home,
uh, at 3:00 or so...

336
00:16:46.206 --> 00:16:50.042
and he was captured by two detectives
in the hallway of his house.

337
00:16:50.043 --> 00:16:54.713
Now, my question is, if he
really had killed his father...

338
00:16:54.714 --> 00:16:58.008
why would he come back
home three hours later?

339
00:16:58.009 --> 00:17:00.970
Wouldn't he be afraid
of being caught?

340
00:17:00.971 --> 00:17:02.805
He came home to get his knife.

341
00:17:02.806 --> 00:17:05.432
It's not nice to go around leaving
knives sticking in people's chests.

342
00:17:05.433 --> 00:17:08.269
- Yeah, especially relatives.
- Yeah.

343
00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:10.604
I don't see
anything funny about it.

344
00:17:10.605 --> 00:17:14.233
The boy knew the knife could be
identified as the one he had just bought.

345
00:17:14.234 --> 00:17:16.485
He had to get it
before the police did.

346
00:17:16.486 --> 00:17:18.767
But if he knew the knife
could be identified,

347
00:17:18.793 --> 00:17:21.073
why did he leave it there
in the first place?

348
00:17:21.491 --> 00:17:24.451
Well, I think we can assume the boy
ran out in a state of panic...

349
00:17:24.452 --> 00:17:26.912
after having just
killed his father.

350
00:17:26.913 --> 00:17:29.957
When he finally calmed down,
he realized he had left his knife there.

351
00:17:29.958 --> 00:17:32.960
Ah. This then depends on
your definition of panic.

352
00:17:32.961 --> 00:17:35.421
He would have had to be
calm enough to see to it...

353
00:17:35.422 --> 00:17:38.090
that there were no fingerprints
left on the knife.

354
00:17:38.091 --> 00:17:42.219
Now, where did this panic
start and where did it end?

355
00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.680
Look, you voted guilty.
What side are ya on?

356
00:17:44.681 --> 00:17:48.309
I don't believe I have to be
loyal to one side or the other.

357
00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.185
I'm simply asking questions.

358
00:17:50.186 --> 00:17:53.272
Well, this is just off the
top of my head, but, uh...

359
00:17:53.273 --> 00:17:55.858
well, if I were the boy and I'd
done the stabbing and everything...

360
00:17:55.859 --> 00:17:58.068
I'd take a chance and
go back for the knife.

361
00:17:58.069 --> 00:18:00.654
I'll bet he figured that nobody
had seen him running out...

362
00:18:00.655 --> 00:18:03.240
and that the body wouldn't be
discovered till the next day.

363
00:18:03.241 --> 00:18:05.367
Well, after all, it was
the middle of the night.

364
00:18:05.368 --> 00:18:07.953
I'll bet he'd figured nobody would
find the body till the next day.

365
00:18:07.954 --> 00:18:10.039
Pardon. This is my whole point.

366
00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:13.834
The woman across the street testified that
the moment after she saw the killing...

367
00:18:13.835 --> 00:18:16.503
that is, a moment after
the train went by...

368
00:18:16.504 --> 00:18:19.590
she screamed and then went
to telephone the police.

369
00:18:19.591 --> 00:18:23.052
Now, the boy certainly must
have heard the scream...

370
00:18:23.053 --> 00:18:26.639
so he knew that
somebody saw something.

371
00:18:26.640 --> 00:18:29.516
I just don't think that
he would have gone back.

372
00:18:29.517 --> 00:18:32.353
Two things. One, in his state of panic,
he may not have heard the scream.

373
00:18:32.354 --> 00:18:34.104
Perhaps it wasn't very loud.

374
00:18:34.105 --> 00:18:37.566
Two, if he did hear it, he may not
have connected it with his own act.

375
00:18:37.567 --> 00:18:40.694
Remember, he lived in a neighborhood
where screams were fairly commonplace.

376
00:18:40.695 --> 00:18:43.238
- There's your answer.
- Maybe.

377
00:18:43.239 --> 00:18:46.200
Maybe the boy did kill his father,
didn't hear the scream...

378
00:18:46.201 --> 00:18:49.995
did run out in a panic,
did calm down three hours later...

379
00:18:49.996 --> 00:18:52.706
and come back to get the knife,
risking being caught by the police.

380
00:18:52.707 --> 00:18:55.709
Maybe all of those things happened,
but maybe they didn't.

381
00:18:55.710 --> 00:18:58.545
I think there's enough doubt that we can
wonder whether he was there at all...

382
00:18:58.546 --> 00:19:00.297
during the time the
killing took place.

383
00:19:00.298 --> 00:19:02.132
What do you mean doubt?
What are you talking about?

384
00:19:02.133 --> 00:19:03.926
Didn't the old man see him
running out of the house?

385
00:19:03.927 --> 00:19:05.803
He's twisting the facts,
I'm telling you.

386
00:19:05.804 --> 00:19:09.223
Did or didn't the old man see the kid
running out of the house at 12:10?

387
00:19:09.224 --> 00:19:11.225
Well, did or didn't he?

388
00:19:11.226 --> 00:19:14.520
- He says he did.
- "Says he did"? Boy! How do you like that?

389
00:19:14.521 --> 00:19:17.564
- Now, look, witnesses can make mistakes.
- Sure, when you want them to, they do.

390
00:19:17.565 --> 00:19:19.900
Or when he wants them to, they do.
You know what I mean?

391
00:19:19.901 --> 00:19:21.819
- Keep the yelling down!
- Oh, you keep saying that.

392
00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:23.612
Maybe what we need
is a little yelling in here.

393
00:19:23.613 --> 00:19:25.406
These guys keep going
off every which way.

394
00:19:25.407 --> 00:19:27.408
Did hear the scream.
Didn't hear the scream.

395
00:19:27.409 --> 00:19:30.661
What difference does it make? You people
are talking only about the little details.

396
00:19:30.662 --> 00:19:33.247
You're forgetting the important stuff.
I mean, all of a sudden, everybody here...

397
00:19:33.248 --> 00:19:35.708
- I want to call for another vote.
- I'm talking here!

398
00:19:35.709 --> 00:19:39.478
There's another vote called for.
Now let's take our seats.

399
00:19:43.258 --> 00:19:45.884
I never saw so much
time spent on nothing.

400
00:19:45.885 --> 00:19:47.694
It only takes a second.

401
00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:52.182
Okay. I guess the fastest way is to
find out who's voting not guilty.

402
00:19:52.183 --> 00:19:55.035
Now, all those voting not guilty,
please raise your hands.

403
00:19:56.187 --> 00:19:59.857
Still the same. One, two,
three not guilty. Nine guilty.

404
00:19:59.858 --> 00:20:01.608
So, now where are we?

405
00:20:01.609 --> 00:20:05.112
I'm telling you, we can yackety-yack until
next Tuesday. Where's it all getting us?

406
00:20:05.113 --> 00:20:07.948
Pardon. I vote not guilty.

407
00:20:07.949 --> 00:20:11.035
Oh!

408
00:20:11.036 --> 00:20:14.413
What are ya talking about? I mean, we're
all going crazy in here or something.

409
00:20:14.414 --> 00:20:18.292
The kid is guilty. Why don't
you listen to the facts?

410
00:20:18.293 --> 00:20:20.627
Tell him, will ya? Now this
is getting to be a joke.

411
00:20:20.628 --> 00:20:23.922
The vote is eight to four,
favor of guilty.

412
00:20:23.923 --> 00:20:27.676
What is this, love your underprivileged
brother week or something?

413
00:20:27.677 --> 00:20:30.262
I want you to get up and tell me
why you changed your vote.

414
00:20:30.263 --> 00:20:34.641
- Come on. Give me your reasons.
- I don't have to defend my decision to you.

415
00:20:34.642 --> 00:20:36.477
There is a reasonable
doubt in my mind.

416
00:20:36.478 --> 00:20:39.563
What reasonable doubt? That's nothing
but words. Here, look at this.

417
00:20:39.564 --> 00:20:44.568
The kid you just decided isn't guilty
was seen ramming this into his father.

418
00:20:44.569 --> 00:20:46.653
Now, what about this,
Mr. Reasonable Doubt?

419
00:20:46.654 --> 00:20:49.406
That's not the knife.
Don't you remember?

420
00:20:49.407 --> 00:20:51.658
Oh, brilliant.

421
00:20:51.659 --> 00:20:54.036
I'm telling ya,
this is the craziest.

422
00:20:54.037 --> 00:20:56.288
I mean, what are we
supposed to believe?

423
00:20:56.289 --> 00:20:59.208
I mean, you're sittin' here, you're pulling
stories out of thin air, you know?

424
00:20:59.209 --> 00:21:03.003
A guy like this, if he's, uh, sitting
ringside at the Dempsey-Firpo fight...

425
00:21:03.004 --> 00:21:05.547
he'd be trying to
tell you that, uh...

426
00:21:05.548 --> 00:21:08.008
Look. Now, what about the old man?

427
00:21:08.009 --> 00:21:10.677
Are we supposed to believe that he
didn't get up and run to his door...

428
00:21:10.678 --> 00:21:13.764
and see the kid tearing down the
steps 15 seconds after the killing?

429
00:21:13.765 --> 00:21:16.308
He's just saying so to
be important, right?

430
00:21:16.309 --> 00:21:17.869
I mean, what's the point
of the whole thing?

431
00:21:17.895 --> 00:21:19.186
Wait. Hold it a
second, will you?

432
00:21:19.187 --> 00:21:21.480
Oh, and the Baltimore rooter
is heard from again now...

433
00:21:21.481 --> 00:21:23.524
and pop-ups are fallin' for
base hits wherever we look.

434
00:21:23.525 --> 00:21:26.860
Hold it a second. Now, look... Did
the old man say he ran to the door?

435
00:21:26.861 --> 00:21:28.780
Ran, walked. What's the difference?
He got on, didn't he?

436
00:21:28.806 --> 00:21:29.905
I mean, he got there, didn't he?

437
00:21:29.906 --> 00:21:32.533
- No, now wait a second.
- He said he ran. At least I think he did.

438
00:21:32.534 --> 00:21:36.036
Look, I don't remember what he said, but I
don't see how he could have run to the door.

439
00:21:36.037 --> 00:21:38.497
He said he went from his
bedroom to the front door.

440
00:21:38.498 --> 00:21:40.207
- Now, isn't that enough?
- Where was the bedroom?

441
00:21:40.208 --> 00:21:42.543
- It was down the hall somewhere.
- I thought you remembered everything.

442
00:21:42.544 --> 00:21:44.878
- Don't you remember that?
- No.

443
00:21:44.879 --> 00:21:47.189
Mr. Foreman, I'd like to see
a diagram of the apartment.

444
00:21:47.215 --> 00:21:49.783
Why don't we just have 'em run the trial all
over again so you can get everything straight?

445
00:21:49.784 --> 00:21:50.509
Mr. Foreman.

446
00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:53.220
How come you're the only one in this
room wants to see exhibits all the time?

447
00:21:53.221 --> 00:21:56.014
- I want to see this one too.
- And I'd like to stop wasting time.

448
00:21:56.015 --> 00:21:58.934
If we're gonna start wading through all that
nonsense about where the body was found...

449
00:21:58.935 --> 00:22:00.978
We're not, not unless
somebody else wants to.

450
00:22:00.979 --> 00:22:02.771
But I'd like to find
out if an old man...

451
00:22:02.772 --> 00:22:05.232
who drags one foot when he walks
'cause he had a stroke last year...

452
00:22:05.233 --> 00:22:07.734
can get from his bedroom to
his front door in 15 seconds.

453
00:22:07.735 --> 00:22:09.486
- He said 20 seconds.
- He said 15.

454
00:22:09.487 --> 00:22:11.738
He said 20 seconds. What are
you trying to distort...

455
00:22:11.739 --> 00:22:15.075
- He said 15.
- How does he know how long 15 seconds is?

456
00:22:15.076 --> 00:22:16.827
You can't judge a thing like that.

457
00:22:16.828 --> 00:22:19.288
He said 15 seconds.
He was very positive about it.

458
00:22:19.289 --> 00:22:21.582
He was an old man.
Half the time he was confused.

459
00:22:21.583 --> 00:22:23.976
How could he be positive
about anything?

460
00:22:31.551 --> 00:22:33.754
I don't see what you're
going to prove here.

461
00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:35.762
The man said he saw
the boy running out.

462
00:22:35.763 --> 00:22:38.015
Well, let's see if the
details bear him out.

463
00:22:38.016 --> 00:22:40.976
As soon as the body hit the floor,
he said he heard footsteps upstairs...

464
00:22:40.977 --> 00:22:42.728
running toward the front door...

465
00:22:42.729 --> 00:22:45.898
heard the upstairs door open,
the footsteps start down the stairs.

466
00:22:45.899 --> 00:22:48.108
He said he got to his own front
door as fast as he could...

467
00:22:48.109 --> 00:22:50.277
and he swore it couldn't have
been more than 15 seconds.

468
00:22:50.278 --> 00:22:52.029
Now, if the killer began
running immediately...

469
00:22:52.030 --> 00:22:54.323
- Maybe he didn't.
- The old man said he did.

470
00:22:54.324 --> 00:22:57.117
You ought to be at Atlantic City
at that hairsplitters convention.

471
00:22:58.328 --> 00:23:00.537
Hey, why don't you stop makin'
smart remarks all the time, huh?

472
00:23:00.538 --> 00:23:03.749
My friend, for your three dollars a day,
you have to listen to everything, you know?

473
00:23:03.750 --> 00:23:05.542
Now that you've got that thing
in here, what about it?

474
00:23:05.543 --> 00:23:07.794
All right. Here's the apartment
where the killing took place.

475
00:23:07.795 --> 00:23:10.923
The old man's apartment was directly
beneath and exactly the same.

476
00:23:10.924 --> 00:23:14.218
Here are the el tracks, the bedroom,
living room, kitchen, bathroom.

477
00:23:14.219 --> 00:23:16.303
Here's the hall.
Here are the stairs.

478
00:23:16.304 --> 00:23:18.639
Now, the old man was in
this bedroom right here.

479
00:23:18.640 --> 00:23:21.391
He says he crossed to the door
and walked down the hall...

480
00:23:21.392 --> 00:23:24.436
opened the front door just in time to
see the boy running down the stairs.

481
00:23:24.437 --> 00:23:27.856
- Am I right so far?
- That's the story, for the 19th time.

482
00:23:27.857 --> 00:23:31.485
- Fifteen seconds after the body hit the floor.
- Correct.

483
00:23:31.486 --> 00:23:33.612
Now, let's see. It was...

484
00:23:33.613 --> 00:23:37.699
It's 12 feet from the bed to
the door. The hall is 43 feet.

485
00:23:37.700 --> 00:23:40.994
He would have had to walk 12 feet,
open the bedroom door...

486
00:23:40.995 --> 00:23:44.706
walk 43 feet down and open the
front door all in 15 seconds.

487
00:23:44.707 --> 00:23:47.501
- Do you think he could have done it?
- Sure he could have done it.

488
00:23:47.502 --> 00:23:50.170
He can walk only very slowly. They had
to help him into the witness chair.

489
00:23:50.171 --> 00:23:51.922
You make it sound like a long walk.

490
00:23:51.923 --> 00:23:54.341
For an old man who had a
stroke, it is a long walk.

491
00:23:54.342 --> 00:23:55.466
Oh! What are you doing?

492
00:23:55.492 --> 00:23:57.861
I'm gonna try it,
see how long it took him.

493
00:23:57.887 --> 00:23:59.236
What do you mean,
you want to try it?

494
00:23:59.237 --> 00:24:01.323
Why didn't his lawyer bring
it up if it's so important?

495
00:24:01.349 --> 00:24:02.606
Maybe he just didn't
think about it, huh?

496
00:24:02.607 --> 00:24:05.060
What do you mean, didn't think of it?
You think the man's an idiot or something?

497
00:24:05.061 --> 00:24:06.687
- It's an obvious thing.
- Did you think of it?

498
00:24:06.688 --> 00:24:08.438
Listen, smart guy. Don't matter
whether I thought of it.

499
00:24:08.439 --> 00:24:10.749
He didn't bring it up because he knew it would
hurt his case. What do you think of that?

500
00:24:10.775 --> 00:24:12.712
Maybe he didn't bring it
up because it would have

501
00:24:12.713 --> 00:24:14.695
meant bullying and badgering
a helpless old man.

502
00:24:14.696 --> 00:24:18.115
You know that doesn't sit very well with
a jury. Most lawyers avoid it if they can.

503
00:24:18.116 --> 00:24:20.909
- So, what kind of a bum is he then?
- That's what I've been asking, buddy.

504
00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:24.037
Pass me that chair, will you?
Those two chairs are the old man's bed.

505
00:24:24.038 --> 00:24:26.999
I just paced off 12 feet across the room.
This will be the bedroom door.

506
00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:29.710
Oh, that's crazy. You can't
recreate a thing like that.

507
00:24:29.711 --> 00:24:32.004
- I'd like to see it.
- The hall was 43 feet.

508
00:24:32.005 --> 00:24:34.106
I'll pace from that
wall and back again.

509
00:24:35.300 --> 00:24:39.219
Look, this is absolutely insane. What are
you wasting everybody's time in here for?

510
00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:42.180
According to you, it'll only take 15
seconds. Now, we can spare that, see.

511
00:24:46.060 --> 00:24:47.811
Come on. Knock it off.

512
00:24:47.812 --> 00:24:49.396
Okay.

513
00:24:49.397 --> 00:24:51.523
Okay. Okay, killer.

514
00:24:51.524 --> 00:24:53.208
Yeah.

515
00:24:55.445 --> 00:24:57.571
Will you stand right there
and mark the front door?

516
00:24:57.572 --> 00:25:00.157
It was chain-locked according
to the testimony, remember?

517
00:25:00.158 --> 00:25:01.927
Has anybody got a watch
with a second hand?

518
00:25:01.953 --> 00:25:02.409
I have.

519
00:25:02.410 --> 00:25:04.911
When you want me to start, stamp
your foot. That'll be the body falling.

520
00:25:04.912 --> 00:25:06.913
You can time me from there.

521
00:25:06.914 --> 00:25:09.082
What are we gonna do,
play charades now?

522
00:25:09.083 --> 00:25:11.209
- Come on! What are we waiting for?
- Okay, I'm ready.

523
00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.211
Well, I want to wait till
the second hand reaches 60.

524
00:25:13.212 --> 00:25:15.230
Oh, come on.

525
00:25:21.846 --> 00:25:24.948
Come on. Speed it up. He could
walk twice as fast as that.

526
00:25:26.309 --> 00:25:28.869
You want me to walk
faster than that, I will.

527
00:25:45.661 --> 00:25:48.205
Lock. Door. Stop.

528
00:25:48.206 --> 00:25:50.290
- Right.
- What's the time?

529
00:25:50.291 --> 00:25:53.627
Uh, exactly 41 seconds.

530
00:25:53.628 --> 00:25:55.962
This is what I think happened.

531
00:25:55.963 --> 00:25:58.882
The old man heard the fight between the
boy and his father a few hours earlier.

532
00:25:58.883 --> 00:26:01.802
Then when he's lying in bed, he heard the
body hit the floor in the boy's apartment...

533
00:26:01.803 --> 00:26:03.635
heard the woman scream
from across the street,

534
00:26:03.661 --> 00:26:05.389
got to his front door
as fast as he could...

535
00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:08.058
heard somebody racing down the
stairs and assumed it was the boy.

536
00:26:08.059 --> 00:26:09.976
- I think that's possible.
- Assumed?

537
00:26:09.977 --> 00:26:12.562
Oh.

538
00:26:12.563 --> 00:26:16.037
Brother, I've seen all kinds
of dishonesty in my day,

539
00:26:16.063 --> 00:26:18.610
but this little display
takes the cake.

540
00:26:18.611 --> 00:26:21.088
You all come in here with
your hearts bleeding all

541
00:26:21.089 --> 00:26:23.407
over the floor about
slum kids and injustice.

542
00:26:23.408 --> 00:26:25.492
You listen to some fairy tales.

543
00:26:25.493 --> 00:26:27.869
Suddenly you start getting through
to some of these old ladies.

544
00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:30.914
Well, you're not getting through
to me. I've had enough.

545
00:26:30.915 --> 00:26:33.041
What's the matter with you guys?

546
00:26:33.042 --> 00:26:35.836
You all know he's guilty!
He's got to burn!

547
00:26:35.837 --> 00:26:37.712
You're letting him slip
through our fingers.

548
00:26:37.713 --> 00:26:40.006
"Slip through our fingers"?
Are you his executioner?

549
00:26:40.007 --> 00:26:41.775
I'm one of 'em!

550
00:26:42.844 --> 00:26:44.761
Perhaps you'd like
to pull the switch.

551
00:26:44.762 --> 00:26:46.847
For this kid, you bet I would.

552
00:26:46.848 --> 00:26:50.200
I feel sorry for you. What it must
feel like to want to pull the switch.

553
00:26:50.977 --> 00:26:53.040
Ever since you walked into
this room, you've been

554
00:26:53.041 --> 00:26:54.980
acting like a self-appointed
public avenger.

555
00:26:54.981 --> 00:26:57.044
You want to see this
boy die because you

556
00:26:57.070 --> 00:26:59.359
personally want it,
not because of the facts.

557
00:26:59.360 --> 00:27:01.611
- You're a sadist.
- You...

558
00:27:01.612 --> 00:27:05.532
Let me go!
I'll kill him. I'll kill him!

559
00:27:05.533 --> 00:27:08.051
You don't really mean
you'll kill me, do you?

560
00:27:15.251 --> 00:27:17.669
Is there anything wrong, gentlemen?
I heard some noise.

561
00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:21.756
Oh, no. Everything's all right. We're just...
You know, friendly little argument. It's...

562
00:27:21.757 --> 00:27:25.151
Um, listen, we're through with that
diagram. You can take it if you want.

563
00:27:26.429 --> 00:27:28.196
Here you are. Thank you.

564
00:27:33.644 --> 00:27:35.829
What are you lookin' at?

565
00:27:56.501 --> 00:27:58.627
I suppose somebody has
to start it off again.

566
00:27:58.628 --> 00:27:59.399
I beg pardon.

567
00:27:59.425 --> 00:28:02.422
"I beg pardon." What
are you so polite about?

568
00:28:02.423 --> 00:28:04.591
For the same reason you're not.

569
00:28:04.592 --> 00:28:06.693
It's the way I was brought up.

570
00:28:08.513 --> 00:28:10.514
This fighting...

571
00:28:10.515 --> 00:28:12.641
That's not why we
are here, to fight.

572
00:28:12.642 --> 00:28:15.118
We have a responsibility.

573
00:28:16.187 --> 00:28:20.857
This, I have always thought, is a
remarkable thing about democracy...

574
00:28:20.858 --> 00:28:22.834
that we are...

575
00:28:23.945 --> 00:28:26.613
Uh, what is the word?

576
00:28:26.614 --> 00:28:29.783
Notified. That we are
notified by mail...

577
00:28:29.784 --> 00:28:34.204
to come down to this place to
decide on the guilt or innocence...

578
00:28:34.205 --> 00:28:38.333
of a man we have never
heard of before.

579
00:28:38.334 --> 00:28:42.979
We have nothing to gain or
lose by... by our verdict.

580
00:28:43.798 --> 00:28:47.025
This is one of the reasons
why we are strong.

581
00:28:48.219 --> 00:28:51.154
We should not make
it a personal thing.

582
00:28:52.390 --> 00:28:54.157
Thank you.

583
00:28:55.268 --> 00:28:57.644
If nobody else has an idea,
I may have a cutie here.

584
00:28:57.645 --> 00:29:00.480
I mean, I haven't given it much thought,
but let's throw it out on the stoop...

585
00:29:00.481 --> 00:29:02.190
and see if the cat licks it up.

586
00:29:02.191 --> 00:29:04.609
- The cat licks it up?
- Yeah. If the boy...

587
00:29:09.031 --> 00:29:11.241
Look how dark it's
getting out there.

588
00:29:11.242 --> 00:29:13.802
I'll bet we're gonna
have a storm now.

589
00:29:15.496 --> 00:29:18.056
Boy, oh, boy. It's really hot, huh?

590
00:29:19.458 --> 00:29:22.352
Pardon me, but don't
you ever sweat?

591
00:29:23.671 --> 00:29:25.922
No, I don't.

592
00:29:25.923 --> 00:29:28.008
Well, listen. I, uh...

593
00:29:28.009 --> 00:29:31.261
I was wondering if, uh, maybe...

594
00:29:31.262 --> 00:29:33.638
maybe we should take another vote.

595
00:29:33.639 --> 00:29:38.018
Oh, great. Maybe we can follow this one up
with a little dancing and refreshments, huh?

596
00:29:38.019 --> 00:29:39.911
- Hmm.
- Mr. Foreman?

597
00:29:40.605 --> 00:29:44.232
Well, that's okay with me.
Anyone doesn't want to vote?

598
00:29:44.233 --> 00:29:47.527
- Hey, come on. Come on.
- I'm sorry.

599
00:29:47.528 --> 00:29:51.114
I think we ought to have an open
ballot... call out our votes, you know.

600
00:29:51.115 --> 00:29:53.408
Let's see who stands where.

601
00:29:53.409 --> 00:29:56.010
That sounds fair to me.
Anyone object?

602
00:29:58.664 --> 00:30:01.474
Okay. I'll call off
your jury numbers. One.

603
00:30:02.376 --> 00:30:05.253
Oh, that's me. I vote guilty.

604
00:30:05.254 --> 00:30:07.130
Two?

605
00:30:07.131 --> 00:30:08.732
Not guilty.

606
00:30:09.175 --> 00:30:12.110
- Number three?
- Guilty.

607
00:30:13.012 --> 00:30:14.654
Number four?

608
00:30:16.057 --> 00:30:17.932
Guilty.

609
00:30:17.933 --> 00:30:20.785
- Number five?
- Not guilty.

610
00:30:21.771 --> 00:30:25.165
- Number six?
- Not guilty.

611
00:30:26.067 --> 00:30:27.817
Number seven?

612
00:30:27.818 --> 00:30:29.611
Guilty.

613
00:30:29.612 --> 00:30:31.738
Number eight?

614
00:30:31.739 --> 00:30:33.506
Not guilty.

615
00:30:34.742 --> 00:30:37.744
- Number nine?
- Not guilty.

616
00:30:37.745 --> 00:30:40.013
- Number 10?
- Guilty.

617
00:30:41.707 --> 00:30:43.833
Number 11?

618
00:30:43.834 --> 00:30:45.752
Not guilty.

619
00:30:45.753 --> 00:30:47.353
Number 12?

620
00:30:48.172 --> 00:30:50.590
Number 12.

621
00:30:50.591 --> 00:30:52.192
Guilty.

622
00:30:58.015 --> 00:31:00.183
The vote is now six to six.

623
00:31:00.184 --> 00:31:03.436
Yeah, right. And we go into
extra innings here, huh?

624
00:31:05.773 --> 00:31:10.360
Six to six. I'm telling you, some of you
people in here must be out of your minds.

625
00:31:10.361 --> 00:31:12.278
A kid like that.

626
00:31:12.279 --> 00:31:15.615
I don't think the kind of boy he
is has anything to do with it.

627
00:31:15.616 --> 00:31:17.784
The facts are supposed
to determine the case.

628
00:31:17.785 --> 00:31:20.036
Don't give me that!
I'm sick and tired of facts!

629
00:31:20.037 --> 00:31:22.122
You can twist them any way you like.
You know what I mean?

630
00:31:22.123 --> 00:31:24.791
That's exactly the point this
gentleman has been making.

631
00:31:24.792 --> 00:31:25.392
Oh!

632
00:31:25.418 --> 00:31:27.794
I mean, you keep shouting
at the top of your lungs.

633
00:31:29.380 --> 00:31:31.381
I'd like to be a few years younger.

634
00:31:31.382 --> 00:31:33.399
That man gets on my...

635
00:31:36.679 --> 00:31:39.597
- My, it's hot in here, isn't it?
- Do you want a drink of water?

636
00:31:39.598 --> 00:31:43.393
No. No, thanks. Thanks.
